    <Comment ID="50082236-582D-47B6-AB94-A1F34A2624C4" Collapsed="No" Color="0.996078 0.964706 0.737255"><![CDATA[{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\uc1\deff0
{\fonttbl{\f0\fmodern\fcharset0\fprq2 Sitka Small;}}
{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\deftab1200\f0\fs20\cf0
\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f0\fs20\b0\i0 ASUS, 31/03/2026 8:09 PM\line \line Come into visual range of the blockade. Need to confirm that this is the lay of events. \line \line We look into cloaking, what\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s feasible, where the lens would be shot from etc. }
\par\plain \f0\fs20\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs20\b0\i0 Leading up to this section, it is still uncertain if there is a section where they discuss the threats in their escape plan? From the lost facility region. \line \line LORE}
\par\plain \f0\fs20\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs20\b0\i0 Outbound concealment doctrine relied on sequence, not invisibility. No vessel could mask an exit burn from traffic control; the plume alone gave away drive class, thrust band, and departure intent. The only viable method was to imitate a legitimate outbound signature until the ship had cleared the atmospheric ascent regime. After that, emissions could be cut and systems run cold. Optical cloaking was reserved for the blockade envelope itself, where surveillance shifted from burn telemetry to visual hull confirmation and volumetric cargo checks.}}]]></Comment>
